{ % vim: set ft=djot : % }

# Djot language server

Build with `cargo build --release --bin djot-lsp`, or install both tools
with `cargo install --path .`.
Run `djot-lsp` to serve the Language Server Protocol over standard input
and output. The server writes only protocol messages to standard output.
The existing `djotfmt` command remains the formatter; `cargo run` still
selects it by default.

## Editor setup

Configure your editor's LSP client to run `djot-lsp` for Djot buffers.
Use language ID `djot` and associate `.dj` and `.djot` files with it.
For example, in Neovim 0.11 or newer:

``` lua
vim.filetype.add({ extension = { dj = 'djot', djot = 'djot' } })
vim.lsp.config('djot', {
  cmd = { 'djot-lsp' },
  filetypes = { 'djot' },
})
vim.lsp.enable('djot')
```

Enable your client's completion UI and snippet support to use link
placeholders. Automatic completion triggering is a client setting; manual
completion also works. Ordinary bracket pairing remains an editor setting.
No custom protocol or editor-specific plugin is required by this server.

## First version

The server supports:

- Whole-document formatting, using the existing formatter.

- A hierarchical heading outline (a flat outline for clients that do not
  support hierarchical document symbols).

- Definition navigation and references within the open document for link
  labels, footnotes, explicit IDs and implicit heading references.

- Completion for link labels after `[text][`, footnotes after `[^`, and
  anchors after `[text](#`.

- Inline-link and reference-link templates after `[`. Accept a candidate
  to insert it; this does not automatically insert text without acceptance.
  Snippet-capable clients can move between the text and destination fields.
  Clients without snippet support receive plain text.

- Standard document links for direct links, reference links, images and
  reference-definition destinations. Relative paths resolve against the
  current document URI; Unicode and spaces are encoded as needed. Your
  client's open-link action can open the target even when it is not already
  open in the editor. External URLs are supported too. Cross-file query
  strings and fragments are preserved; target headings are not analyzed.
  Same-document anchors continue to use definition navigation.

- Warnings for unresolved references and duplicate definitions or IDs.

Completion suppresses suggestions in code, math and parsed attributes.
Reference completions replace an existing closing bracket and can remove
empty inline-link punctuation left by a link template.

Formatting defaults to 72 columns. Clients can pass a positive integer
`maxColumns` in formatting options to change it. The LSP indentation option
`tabSize` is not used as a line-width setting.

## Scope and implementation

This version analyzes open documents independently. It does not scan a
workspace, analyze symbols across files, offer rename, or validate
filesystem paths. An incomplete delimiter alone is not reported as a syntax error.

Each document version is analyzed in full. Its snapshot retains source,
parser events, headings, definitions, references and document links. Context is calculated
on demand from events and nearby source; there is no complete AST or stored
context tree. Analysis uses half-open UTF-8 byte ranges internally, while
the protocol adapter uses UTF-16 positions. Parser positions referring to
the synthetic final newline are clamped to the actual source.

The protocol adapter uses `tower-lsp-server` 0.23.0. The analysis library
has no dependency on that framework's types or runtime. Document updates
are applied before notification handlers yield, and requests take immutable
snapshots. Diagnostics carry document versions. Parsing and analysis are
currently synchronous; large-document scheduling is a later optimization.

## Validation

`cargo test --release` runs parser conformance tests, formatter tests, and
stdio protocol tests covering document analysis and position-encoding
negotiation. Parser conformance needs the pinned
djot.js submodule and Node.js; its harness builds the reference when needed.
The Rust server has no JavaScript runtime dependency.
